The '''1958 FIFA World Cup final''' took place in [[Råsunda Stadium]], [[Solna Municipality|Solna]] (near [[Stockholm]]), Sweden, on 29 June 1958 to determine the champion of the [[1958 FIFA World Cup]].<ref name=planet>{{cite web |url=http://www.planetworldcup.com/CUPS/1958/wc58final.html |title=World Cup 1958: Final |publisher=Planetworldcup.com |access-date=23 May 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20031025165627/http://www.planetworldcup.com/CUPS/1958/wc58final.html |archive-date=25 October 2003 |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Brazil national football team|Brazil]] won the World Cup by defeating [[Sweden men's national football team|Sweden]], and thus won their [[List of FIFA World Cup finals|first World Cup title]]. Despite losing, the game remains Sweden’s best ever World Cup finish.

The 1958 final holds the [[FIFA World Cup records and statistics#Most goals scored in a final match|record]] for most goals scored in a World Cup Final, and it shares the record for the greatest winning margin (with the 1970 and 1998 tournaments). The records for both the [[FIFA World Cup records and statistics#Youngest goalscorer in a final|youngest]] and [[FIFA World Cup records and statistics#Oldest goalscorer at the knock-out round|oldest goalscorer]] in a World Cup final were set in this match by [[Pelé]] (17 years and 249 days) and [[Nils Liedholm]] (35 years, 263 days) respectively.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://olympics.com/en/news/fifa-world-cup-final-records-stats-faqs|title=FIFA World Cup final: Records, stats and FAQs|date=18 December 2022|access-date=20 December 2022|website=Olympics|archive-date=20 December 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221220210049/https://olympics.com/en/news/fifa-world-cup-final-records-stats-faqs|url-status=dead}}</ref> The final also marked several firsts: It was the first final to be disputed between a European team and a team from the Americas. Sweden became the first, and so far, only host to lose a World Cup Final (the [[Uruguay v Brazil (1950 FIFA World Cup)|Maracanazo]] of 1950 was the decisive match of the tournament, but was not a 'Final', because [[Sweden men's national football team|Sweden]] v [[Spain national football team|Spain]] [[1950 FIFA World Cup final round#Sweden vs Spain|was played simultaneously]]). Their loss also meant that for the only time a World Cup staged in Europe was not won by a European nation.

The last survivor on Brazil's side was [[Mário Zagallo]], who died on 5 January 2024 at the age of 92.<ref>{{cite web |date=6 January 2024 |title=Morre Zagallo, uma das lendas do futebol brasileiro |url=https://g1.globo.com/rj/rio-de-janeiro/noticia/2024/01/06/morre-zagallo.ghtml |access-date=6 January 2024 |website=[[G1 (website)|G1]] |publisher=[[Grupo Globo]] |language=pt-BR |archive-date=27 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250427020254/https://g1.globo.com/rj/rio-de-janeiro/noticia/2024/01/06/morre-zagallo.ghtml |url-status=live }}</ref> Nearly a month later, the last survivor of the game, Sweden's [[Kurt Hamrin]], died on 4 February 2024 at the age of 89.<ref>{{cite web |date=4 February 2024 |title=È morto Kurt Hamrin, addio alla leggenda della Fiorentina |url=https://www.lanazione.it/firenze/sport/fiorentina/kurt-hamrin-xq4j75xg |access-date=4 February 2024 |website=[[La Nazione]] |language=it |archive-date=4 February 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240204100345/https://www.lanazione.it/firenze/sport/fiorentina/kurt-hamrin-xq4j75xg |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Match==

===Background===
Since both teams wore a yellow kit as their first choice, a draw was arranged in order to decide which team would use its regular strip. Brazil boycotted the draw, thus making Sweden winner, and forcing Brazil to find another color to wear. Initially, Brazil was going to wear white, but this idea was rejected when the players were visibly frightened by the idea, recalling their [[Uruguay v Brazil (1950 FIFA World Cup)|loss in 1950]].<ref>{{cite book |last1=Kraba |first1=Milile |title=The Story Has Been Told |date=10 September 2010 |publisher=Xlibris Corporation |page=60 |isbn=9781453566107 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=gNuoU8qgR_YC&pg=PA60}}</ref> Eventually the staff went on to buy 22 blue T-shirts and sewed the Brazilian emblem.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/magazine-27809268|title=The story of Brazil's 'sacred' yellow and green jersey|date=28 June 2014|access-date=2 July 2020|work=BBC News|archive-date=3 December 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221203122616/https://www.bbc.com/news/magazine-27809268|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Summary===
Sweden took the lead after only 4 minutes after an excellent finish by captain [[Nils Liedholm]]. The lead did not last long, as [[Vavá]] equalised just 5 minutes later. On 32 minutes, Vavá scored a similar goal to his first to give Brazil a lead 2–1 at the break. 10 minutes into the second half, Brazil went further in front thanks to a brilliant goal scored by [[Pelé]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/newscentre/news/newsid=82406/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120523231346/http://www.fifa.com/newscentre/news/newsid=82406/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=23 May 2012 |title=Diego Maradona goal voted the FIFA World Cup Goal of the Century |work=FIFA.com |publisher=[[FIFA|Fédération Internationale de Football Association]] |date=30 May 2002 |access-date=8 May 2011}}</ref> He took control of the ball inside the penalty area, chipped the ball over the defender then smashed it past a helpless [[Kalle Svensson]]. Halfway through the second half Brazil went 4–1 up with a goal scored by [[Mário Zagallo]]. [[Agne Simonsson|Simonsson]] pulled one back for Sweden with 10 minutes remaining but it was far too late.<ref name=planet/> Pelé sealed the 5–2 victory for Brazil with a headed goal in stoppage time.

